RULES & REGULATIONS

Fair and Consistent Competition

The Obstacle Course Racing Association of Ireland (OCRA ÉIREANN) supports fair, safe and consistent competition across obstacle sport in Ireland.

Competition rules help ensure that athletes understand what is expected of them and that Technical Officials can apply the same standards consistently across a competition.

Competition Rules

Different obstacle sport disciplines can require different rules.

Depending on the event, rules may cover:

- Race format
- Starting procedures
- Course completion
- Obstacle completion
- Mandatory techniques
- Obstacle retries
- Penalties
- Failed obstacles
- Timing
- Equipment
- Categories
- Age Groups
- Athlete conduct
- Technical Officials
- Protests and appeals
- Disqualification
- Result classifications

Discipline-Specific Rules

Different formats of obstacle racing test athletes in different ways.

OCRA will publish the current rules applicable to competitions it organises or sanctions.

These may include specific rules for disciplines such as:

- 100m
- 400m
- Short Course
- Standard Course
- Team and Relay competition
- Other recognised obstacle sport formats

Obstacle Completion and Penalties

Competitive obstacle racing may require athletes to complete obstacles according to specific rules.

Depending on the competition, an athlete may be required to:

- Complete an obstacle successfully
- Follow a defined obstacle technique
- Remain within a designated lane
- Complete or repeat a specific section
- Accept a penalty following obstacle failure

The exact procedure will depend on the rules of the particular discipline and competition.

Athletes should always review the applicable rulebook before racing.

Technical Officials

Technical Officials are responsible for observing competition and applying the applicable rules consistently and impartially.

Athletes are expected to follow instructions given by authorised Technical Officials during competition.

Information about becoming a Technical Official can be found in the Education section of the website.

Protests and Appeals

Some competitive events provide athletes with a formal process to challenge a decision or result.

Where a protest or appeal procedure applies, athletes will be informed of:

- What decisions may be challenged
- Who can submit a protest
- How a protest must be submitted
- The applicable time limit
- Supporting evidence requirements
- Who will review the protest
- Whether a further appeal is available

Championship-specific protest procedures may apply.

International Competition

International championships may operate under rules issued by the applicable international governing organisation or championship organiser.

OCRA will provide access to relevant current competition rules for Irish athletes where available.

Athletes competing internationally are responsible for familiarising themselves with the rules applying to their championship.

Current Rulebooks

Only current competition rulebooks should be presented as active rules.

Each rulebook should clearly display:

- Discipline
- Version
- Effective date

Archived Rulebooks

Previous rulebooks may remain available for historical reference.

Archived documents should be clearly identified as no longer current.

This is particularly important where rules have changed between competition seasons.

